<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    Surfaces — the boxes.

    A surface exists only when its content is independently actionable. Grouping alone is a header plus
    spacing, not a box; a border drawn round things that merely belong together adds a line the eye has to
    resolve and buys nothing. That rule is why this file is short and should stay short.

    Elevation, four levels, one shadow:

      L0  the Mica backdrop. Nothing opaque is painted over it, or the backdrop stops being visible and the
          window stops looking like a Windows window.
      L1  grouped content — a settings group, a section of the About page.
      L2  an interactive card: a console tile, a discovered-console row.
      L3  transient over content — a flyout, a dialog, a panel over the video. THE ONLY place ThemeShadow
          appears.

    L2 NEVER gets a drop shadow. Windows 11 separates cards with stroke and fill, and a shadowed card is the
    loudest "this app came from somewhere else" tell there is. If a card is not reading as separate, the fix
    is its fill or its stroke, never a shadow.

    In-app acrylic belongs to L3 and only over content genuinely behind it — over video correct, over Mica
    wrong, because double-blurring goes muddy. When the user has transparency off, every acrylic here must
    fall back to a solid brush; that is decided in code, since a dictionary cannot read the setting.
-->
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--  L1/L2 content card. From AboutPage.CardStyle / SettingsPage.SettingsCardStyle, which were identical.  -->
    <Style x:Key="RipcordCardStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource CardBackgroundFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource CardStrokeColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RipcordCardCornerRadius}" />
    </Style>

    <!--  A small rounded label for a single fact: version, licence, support level. From AboutPage.ChipStyle.  -->
    <Style x:Key="RipcordChipStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource ControlFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource ControlStrokeColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RipcordPillCornerRadius}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,2" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!--  A state pill. From SessionPage.CapabilityPillStyle — tighter padding and a dimmer fill than a chip.  -->
    <Style x:Key="RipcordPillStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource ControlFillColorSecondaryBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource ControlStrokeColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RipcordPillCornerRadius}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="8,2" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!--  The one capability worth the accent fill, so the row has a headline rather than four equal tokens.  -->
    <Style
        x:Key="RipcordAccentPillStyle"
        BasedOn="{StaticResource RipcordPillStyle}"
        TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A card whose content is a row rather than a layout of its own, so the padding belongs to the card. The
        settings page is all of these; the About page's cards arrange their own interiors and take the bare
        card above instead.
    -->
    <Style
        x:Key="RipcordRowCardStyle"
        BasedOn="{StaticResource RipcordCardStyle}"
        TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="16,12" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        A hairline between rows of a table. A divider, not a surface: it exists because the rows are a single
        Grid rather than a card each, which is what keeps the labels aligned in one column whatever their
        length — including at the larger text sizes the Display settings offer.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="RipcordRowDividerStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="1" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource DividerStrokeColorDefaultBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        One step's dash in a progress indicator: filled once reached, faded by the caller before. Shared rather
        than owned by the pairing flow because "a short run of dashes showing how far through you are" is a
        pattern, not a page — a first-run or account-setup flow wants the identical mark, and a second copy is
        how two indicators end up different heights.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="RipcordStepDashStyle" TargetType="Rectangle">
        <Setter Property="Height" Value="4" />
        <Setter Property="RadiusX" Value="2" />
        <Setter Property="RadiusY" Value="2" />
        <Setter Property="Fill" Value="{ThemeResource AccentFillColorDefaultBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        ================================================================================================
        Focus engagement is off, app-wide

        A Slider defaults to IsFocusEngagementEnabled="True": directional focus will not leave it until the
        control has been explicitly "engaged" and then disengaged, and both of those are defined purely in
        terms of gamepad key events — GamepadA, GamepadB and friends arriving through KeyDown.

        In a WinUI 3 DESKTOP app those events never arrive at all. There is no CoreWindow, so no gamepad
        virtual key is ever delivered; this app reads the pad itself and calls the focus engine directly.
        Engagement therefore can never be entered and, crucially, never exited — so the control is not merely
        awkward with a pad, it is a one-way trap. Measured: pressing Down on the bitrate slider left focus on
        the bitrate slider, forever, with no keyboard on a handheld to escape with.

        Turned off as an implicit style rather than per instance so the next slider anyone adds inherits
        whatever the eventual fix turns out to be. Alongside it, FocusPilot routes the axes: Left/Right adjust
        the value, Up/Down are meant to move focus — the same split the arrow keys already use, so there is no
        second mental model and no engaged/disengaged state to get stuck in.

        DOES NOT ACTUALLY WORK YET, and this comment is the warning rather than the record of a fix. Turning
        engagement off did not release directional focus, and neither did FocusPilot's tab-order fallback;
        pressing Down on the bitrate slider still leaves focus on the bitrate slider. B (navigate back) is the
        only escape today. So the real cause is NOT engagement, or not only engagement — it is still unknown,
        and this setter is kept because it is correct in intent, not because it was observed to help. Tracked
        in ROADMAP. Anyone picking this up: start by checking whether the Slider's own template is handling
        the directional move before FindNextElement is ever consulted.
        ================================================================================================
    -->
    <Style TargetType="Slider">
        <Setter Property="IsFocusEngagementEnabled" Value="False" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
